deps-restorer, install: name a replaced tree that can't be deleted, and sweep it later (homescoop#1532)

0010/0011 rename the old tree aside, rename the staged one in, and then
delete the old one. When that delete fails (an entry pnpm can't unlink, such
as a file in a read-only directory), the old tree stayed as a
<name>_pacquet-stage_* sibling with only a tracing event no reporter shows,
and no later install ever looked at it. Now the failure is named on stderr
and the leftover's path is recorded in the outermost node_modules'
.pacquet-stage-leftovers. Every install that touches node_modules (not
--lockfile-only or --dry-run) sweeps the root's and each project's
node_modules first, before the repeat-install check can end the run: the
recorded leftovers, and unrecorded *_pacquet-stage_* entries directly in
node_modules or a scope directory (what earlier builds left). One that still
can't be deleted is named again and stays recorded; an unrecorded one holding
a node_modules is named and kept, because a failed swap may have set
preserved dependencies aside there. Recorded paths are relative, must stay
below the node_modules and carry the stage mark.

Upstream: not filed (Lars decides).

